A||N||5||Anyhony||need to lower my fiero 3" in the rear||||1
Z||000000||Anyhony||10-28-1999||12:58 AM||||I need to lower my 86 fiero 3" in the rear is this possible with those coil over shocks offerd by some companies,and if so will it efect my handling in any way||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shiner||10-28-1999||04:04 AM||||I can tell you this will not affect your handling in the SLIGHTEST bit.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-28-1999||07:36 AM||||Coil over set-ups in the rear are nice because they are completely adjustable and the springs are easily swapped in and out for different ride height or spring rate. I've listed a couple of websites that offer this:<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html</A> <P> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/fiero.htm</A> <P>Ryane's website claims to have a 15% sale going on until October 31 and mentioned that they were considering halting the production on their Fiero based products.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||10-28-1999||07:46 AM||||I've lowered the arse end of my kitcar 2" with the Raynes setup and I could go about another 1.5" if I needed to. The car is not roadable yet but just testing it up and down the street all seems to be just fine. But I think lowering 3" might be too much because you would have to lower the front by around the same amount and you could run into tire clearance problems over bumps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||10-28-1999||12:53 PM||||If I lowered my 86GT or 87SE 3 inches, I would have to drive around gum wrappers that are in the street. Both cars are stock, maybe I just have a low profile tires? One of my floor jacks will not even go under them now. I wouldn't jump at the three inches off the get-go Maybe try half that first?? Just my thoughts. Have others out there lowered theirs that much?<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000005||lowCG||10-28-1999||04:54 PM||||Good luck.<BR>Well actually,there's some rubber dog toys from England that look a heck of alot like Austin Mini rubber springs.They look like three spheres that intersect each other inline,with a hole down through the middle.The bumpstop from hell;just use a pair of these,forget the springs,you won't have any travel left anyways if you go that low.<BR>If you go that low in front,your geometry will be so screwed up,you'll get blown away by Pintos,Chevettes,and Dodge Darts.<BR>Racecars are built with the control arm mountings up higher,that's how they do it successfully.<BR>With the control arms pointing up,you've moved the point that the car leans around(roll center)downwards,creating a situation where the car will pitch around more(more weight transfer,bad),and throw your head from side to side over bumps.Of course you could just make it really stiff,and put sticky tires on,like a go-cart.||||reg||1
